At the time of writing, there are just over 200 known zeolite topol­ ogies, and they are strictly defined as comprising tetrahedrally con­ nected building units. The most important class of zeolite consists of the high-silica materials, and there are far fewer of these (around 40–50). All the industrially important framework topologies (MFI, FER, CHA and others) are members of this class or can be made as high silica. Many attempts have been made to enumerate the hypo­ thetically possible topologies, by calculating the number of ways that silica tetrahedra can be linked to form fully connected structures. There are now several different databases with several million possibil­ ities identified (see for example http://www.hypotheticalzeolites.net and http://mezeopor.jlu.edu.cn)5,6. This leads to the ‘zeolite conun­ drum’: why have we only been able to make such a small subset of possible zeolite structures7? There are many more MOF structures than zeolites, but that is a consequence of the vast variety of com­ positions possible. If we restrict our thoughts to tetrahedrally con­ nected MOF materials with zeolite-like topologies (of which the most common are a subset called $\mathrm{ZIFs^{8}}\mathrm{,}$ , we see a similar paucity of synthesized structures compared with hypothetical ones. It is likely (although not yet definite) that other MOF families will suffer from the same issues.
Several researchers have postulated reasons that only such a small set of zeolites is possible. Akporiyae9 and Cheetham and colleagues10,11 noted that the known, synthesized zeolite structures seem to fit a strong correlation between framework energy and density. Subsequently, a similar correlation was noted for the $Z\mathrm{IF}s^{12}$ (Fig.  1a). Using this information, several groups have proposed criteria as to what mate­ rials are synthetically feasible. These include a ‘feasibility factor’ that is essentially a measure of how far a hypothetical zeolite lies off the energy–density correlation13, and the LID (local interatomic distance) criterion which suggests that only almost ideal topologies in terms of local bond distances and angles are possible synthetic targets14. But the flaw in these approaches is that they are based only on the nature of the final material and do not take into account the pathways needed to reach the final solids, which is equally important to the outcome.
Deem and co-workers5,6, on the other hand, have enumerated more than 300,000 potentially accessible pure silica zeolite struc­ tures (Fig. 1b), using the simple (possibly arbitrary) criterion that the framework energy of these solids should be less than $30\mathrm{k}]$ J per mole of silicon above that of quartz. Deem made the very interesting observation that all the known silica zeolites fit close to the left-hand (low-density) edge of the possible zeolite structures (the energy– density correlation is drawn as the red line in Fig. 1b)5. His explana­ tion for this is based on the limitations of conventional solvothermal synthesis15. In traditional solvothermal synthesis, the density with respect to the silicon in the system is always increasing, from a lowdensity solution or gel phase to the final crystalline material. Deem’s argument is that there are so many zeolite structures possible that as soon as the system reaches a density with respect to silicon that is within the distribution, a zeolite material forms. This is why all known zeolites lie on the left-hand edge of the distribution, and all those lying significantly to the right of the red line are inaccessible to current methods. Deem also uses this diagram to explain why there are very few zeolites with odd-numbered rings greater than five (that is, a ring in the structure containing more than five tetra­ hedral units). The vast majority of the seven-, nine- and eleven-ring zeolites do not lie on the low-density edge, and so are less easy to access using the traditional synthesis approaches15.
If the feasibility of most zeolite materials not only depends on the intrinsic energetics on the framework but is also determined by the limitations of the traditional synthesis method, then we need to develop new synthetic approaches. In the remainder of this
Perspective, we will explore an alternative approach to synthesis of porous materials that makes use of regioselective weakness in a material as a tool that has the potential to avoid such limitations.
## Using weakness as a synthetic tool
In molecular synthetic chemistry, knowing where there are ‘weak­ nesses’ in bonding is the key to most successful chemical preparations. Understanding the conditions needed to change functional groups or to make/break bonds forms the cornerstone of all synthetic chemis­ try, and organic synthesis in particular. The same could be true for materials synthesis if bonds could be selectively broken and remade to manipulate the properties of the materials. The challenge, however, is how to control the location (regioselectivity) of any weakness while also making sure that the structure of the material is not compro­ mised — that is, that the structure of the solid does not collapse. The infinite three-dimensional nature of the materials means that the key is to have some weaker units in the structure at controlled locations, while stronger bonding holds the solid together. In particular, the symmetric arrangement of these weaker bonds will play an impor­ tant part in determining what further manipulations are possible. In recent years this type of approach has led to some startling results in the formation of nanoporous materials with unusual properties.
The manipulation of solid structures to provide new materials with improved functionality is a burgeoning area. Often the types of manipulation used are rather chemically unselective. A good exam­ ple of this would be the preparation of hierarchical materials by non-selective dissolution in basic media to improve diffusion into particles with the goal of increasing catalytic activity16, or the use of ion exchange to prepare new lithium battery materials17. But more sophisticated routes to new materials have recently been realized through the selective engineering of weaker bonds into a solid, often during the original synthetic procedures, which then allow regio­ specific changes in structure on exposure to a stimulus. Examples of mechanical, chemical or physical (for example high pressure) stimuli can be found in the recent literature and are discussed below.
## Metal–organic frameworks
Metal–organic frameworks or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) are arguably some of the most exciting new materials to have been prepared in the past couple of decades3,4. They are composed of met­ als or metal clusters linked into multidimensional networks through multidentate organic linkers. Their properties have elicited great excitement in the community, much of it around their very high surface areas18,19 but increasingly also around the flexibility and ‘soft­ ness’ of $\mathrm{MOFs^{20,21}}$ . One mechanism of flexibility in MOFs requires the linkages in the structure to have fairly flat energy surfaces with respect to changes in interatomic angles22. Good examples are the ‘breathing’ in MIL- $.53^{23}$ and related materials, and the protein-like folding of amino-acid-containing $\mathrm{MOFs^{24}}$ .
In general, MOFs are well known for being rather too sensitive to their environment. Instability towards attack by moisture in particu­ lar is a hindrance in many potential applications, and much research is targeted at increasing their stability25,26. Alternatively, there are now examples of flexibility in MOFs where weaker bonds are broken and remade selectively during changes in topology, and this has led to some interesting and potentially very useful properties. Approaches that fit broadly into this category include a wide range of postsyn­ thetic modification, which in most cases makes use of the organic chemistry possibilities associated with the organic linkers used in $\mathrm{MOF}s^{27,28}$ . Other interesting aspects of this approach include those where the weakness is identified as the coordination bond that holds the linker to the metal. Exploiting this weakness can lead to selective replacement of either the ligand or the metal in a MOF material29–31 and the potential to produce catalytic sites based on defects32,33.
Perhaps one of the most interesting and surprising examples of this type of approach is the use of pressure to form a porous material from a non-porous, denser solid. At first sight, it seems counterin­ tuitive that a less dense phase could be prepared using high pres­ sure. Chapman and co-workers34 prepared an interpenetrated MOF based on zinc cyanide, consisting of two independent networks that are entangled (Fig. 2a). The space filling of the interpenetration is not optimal, and application of hydrostatic pressure, using pres­ sure-transmitting fluids, leads to a situation where the interactions between fluid and framework outweigh the framework–framework interactions; it eventually becomes favourable for one of the two entangled networks to break down, leaving an open framework with the pores filled by the fluid. Releasing the pressure removes the occluded fluid, leaving the porous material, whose exact nature depends on the pressure-transmitting fluid used (Fig. 2a).
The above examples all show no particular regioselectivity in the weak bonds, and the weak bonds in the starting material are distrib­ uted symmetrically. One of the great advantages of MOFs is the wide range of chemistry that can be included in the organic linkers, meaning that ‘designing’ materials with weak bonds should be possible. Given that the key interaction in MOFs is the coordinate bond, one way to develop the concept is to have linkers containing functional groups of different metal-binding ability. A good example is the sulfoisophtha­ late linker, which contains two carboxylate groups that bind relatively strongly to metals and a sulfonate that binds more weakly. Combining this linker with copper leads to a material called Cu-SIP-3 (Fig. 2b), whose most interesting feature is that the bonds between the metal and the sulfonate group can be broken and remade through the choice of conditions35–37. Most importantly, the metal–sulfonate bond can only be broken by ligands that can displace this weak bond, which in turn leads to very selective adsorption only of gases that bind strongly to the metal, including nitric oxide. No other gases tested are adsorbed at all by the material (Fig. 2b)35. The solid also shows some interesting mechanistic features of this process that were elucidated by X-ray dif­ fraction36 and pair distribution function analysis37. Similar properties of other mixed sulfonate/carboxylate ligands have been shown by var­ ious researchers since this initial publication38,39. These materials have been coined ‘hemilabile MOFs’ by analogy with molecular catalysts where bidentate ligands can show similar properties of hemilability on dehydration/rehydration or interaction with other ligands.
An alternative to controlling the reactivity and bonding in the linker is to have flexibility of the binding at the metal centre. A good example of this is the recent work of Kitagawa and co-workers40 in which changes in bonding at the copper centre in a 2D MOF led to some extraordinary adsorption of carbon monoxide, dubbed selfaccelerating behaviour. The key to this property is the rearrange­ ment of bonds around the copper metal as shown in Fig. 2c, which then leads to significant changes in the pore structure of the mate­ rial. A solid with a similar topology, named STAM-1, also shows some interesting effects on dehydration/rehydration, leading to a material where the location of adsorption can be switched between different pores in the structure (‘switchable adsorption’)41.
## Zeolites
Whereas MOFs have the great advantage of the possibility of design­ ing the linker chemistry by using organic synthesis methodologies and exploiting the weak nature of the coordination bonding, purely inorganic materials such as zeolites look, at first glance, as if they offer no such opportunities. All the bonds in a pure silica zeolite are essentially of the same type and inevitably almost indistinguishable. The only possibility for distinguishing them comes from the topol­ ogy of the material when subtle strain, especially in small rings, can be introduced. Chemical flexibility in zeolites then arises only from the possibility of replacing silicon with other elements, especially if this can be completed in a regioselective fashion.
Boron. Aluminosilicate zeolites are, of course, extremely well known42, but the location of aluminium siting is difficult to con­ trol and the Al–O and the Si–O bonds are both relatively strong. Although it is possible to dealuminate zeolites43,44, there is little control over the regioselectivity of this process. Further manipula­ tion of other zeotypes, such as borosilicates, has been accomplished with more success. In these cases, the boron can be removed from the zeolite structure and replaced by more useful elements, notably aluminium45.  This is especially useful for those materials that can only be prepared as borosilicates and not aluminosilicates. Given the relatively low cost of boron, this type of synthesis approach has attracted much attention, and it offers ways to prepare new zeolite catalysts. A particularly interesting feature is that exploitation of the weakness manifested in boron-containing zeolite is dependent on the types of channel present, and those sites present in large chan­ nels are much more susceptible to replacement by other atoms than those in smaller channels. This provides opportunities to develop completely new types of catalysts. A good example of this potential is the selective alumination of only the large channels in SSZ-57, which is the most complex zeolite to have been fully structurally characterized46. This material is disordered, but contains about $6\%$ of large channels. Boron can be incorporated into the solid during synthesis, but only the boron in the large channels is then susceptible to replacement by aluminium, using a solution of $\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ (ref. 47). This leads to a catalyst with unusual properties that are determined by the specific location of the aluminium, control over which was only possible through exploiting the weakness of the boron.
Another interesting example is the delamination of zeolites under mild conditions using boron. Delaminated (sometimes called two-dimensional, 2D) zeolites are of great current inter­ est48,49 because they can offer better accessibility to catalytic sites, reducing diffusion issues50. Many delamination approaches are plagued by very strong conditions of high acidity/basicity and the presence of surfactant. Using deboronation as a step in the delami­ nation process can lead to successful delamination under much milder conditions51,52. The external surface area (as measured by adsorption) and catalytic activity of the materials increase signifi­ cantly after such a treatment, illustrating the improved materials properties that are possible.
Germanium. Germanium is another element that can be used as a dopant/structure-directing agent in the synthesis of zeolites. As a synthesis technique, the synthesis of germanosilicate zeolites has been spectacularly successful in providing new structures, espe­ cially with larger pores53,54. Germanium tends to produce large pores because it favours the formation of small rings that are slightly strained in pure silica materials, in particular the doublefour-ring (D4R) units and the single- and double-three-rings (S3R and D3R respectively)55. In turn, it is predicted computationally that the smaller the rings in the zeolite structure, the lower the density of the material, which generally means larger pore sizes56. The Corma group in Spain has been particularly active in this area producing and characterizing novel large-pore solids using this approach53,57. It has also been proven in several experimental stud­ ies that the germanium is, in most materials, preferentially located within the small rings58,59.
The use of germanium has, however, some perceived drawbacks. First, of course, is the greater expense associated with germanium when compared with silicon. Second, germanium oxide bonds have more reactivity towards moisture, making zeolites prepared with significant amounts of germanium less stable than their silica or aluminosilicate counterparts. Corma has therefore suggested that to prepare any economically viable and stable germanosilicate requires the Si/Ge ratio to be larger than 200, ruling out many of the interest­ ing topologies that have yet to be prepared as Ge-poor materials55. Recently, however, this perceived weakness has been turned into an opportunity by researchers who have noticed how the reactivity of germanium can be turned into a useful feature. The first class of this type is similar to the use of boron described above. Under the right conditions, germanium can be removed from the zeolite (degerma­ nation) and replaced by other elements, in particular silicon and aluminium. This is therefore a route to developing materials that are more stable towards water than the germanosilicates and have strong acidic sites that may lead to useful catalytic materials. This does not necessarily require any regioselectivity in the germanium siting, but because it often does site preferentially in small rings this route offers a route to knowing exactly where the Al sites are, some­ thing that is often difficult to ascertain in other zeolites (assuming, of course, that the Al substitutes directly for the germanium without any rearrangement). Tuel and co-workers have been instrumental in understanding where the germanium is located in the zeolite and how the degermanation process works60–63.
Combining the preferential, regioselective siting of germanium with its reactivity towards water offers further possibilities, and in particular understanding how the weakness in the material can be used to manipulate the structure of a material selectively to lead to entirely new materials. The inverse sigma transformation route is an example of such an approach64. The germanosilicate with the struc­ ture that has the International Zeolite Association (IZA) topology code UTL (IM-12 (ref. 65) or ITQ-15 (ref. 66); see iza-online.org for further details of topology codes) comprises layers of silica con­ nected by D4R units that contain both germanium and Si. Verheyen et al.64 used a very highly concentrated HCl solution (12 M) to selec­ tively remove only the Ge atoms from the D4R rings that separate the layers and forming a new zeolite material called COK-14 (IZA code OKO). Structural studies, using EXAFS (extended X-ray absorption fine structure) and X-ray diffraction, of the intermediates formed during the process indicate that the mechanism may proceed by way of a concerted removal of a four ring from the original IM-12 material followed by formation of a new material that only has the silicon-containing single ring S4R between the layers. The overall result is therefore a reduction by half of the number of atoms that link the layers, hence the term inverse sigma transformation.
The same combination of regioselective location and hydrolytic sensitivity of germanium in UTL-type zeolites can be used to pre­ pare a whole family of new zeolites using the ‘assembly–disassem­ bly–organization–reassembly’ (ADOR) method67–74. Hydrolysis of the starting materials leads to selective removal (de-intercalation) of the whole D4R unit between the layers to form a material called IPC-1P (ref. 67). Calcination of this material leads to the forma­ tion of a new zeolite called IPC-4 (IZA code PCR). Reintroducing more silicon between the layers by intercalating diethoxydi­ methylsilane into the solid, followed by calcination, produces a material, IPC-2, with the silicon-containing S4R between the lay­ ers, with a similar topology to the OKO material described above but different stability and structural features. In this way, a family of new materials can be prepared using the ADOR method, taking advantage of the weakness inherent in the germanium-containing starting materials.
The mechanism of the ADOR process also offers opportuni­ ties to complete other interesting manipulations. Careful studies of how the reaction proceeds shows that it is a two-step process: a de-intercalation that removes the unit from between the layers, and a rearrangement that can build up connecting units between the layers70. By controlling the rates of the two processes, one can control the final outcome of the reaction, leading to precise control over a wide range of different materials with continuously tunable pore sizes (Fig. 3). Such control is not yet possible using traditional methods of synthesis, and it demonstrates how new approaches to synthesis can lead to hitherto unknown properties. The ADOR process looks to be a fairly general method of manipulation of ger­ manosilicates, with recent work showing how it can be applied to other zeolites71,72.
Other heteroatoms in zeolites. One of the great advantages of zeo­ lites is the wealth of possible substitution chemistry that is possible — almost any atom that can be tetrahedrally coordinated has the possibility to be substituted into a zeolite. Boron and germanium are two examples, but other elements such as titanium, zinc, beryl­ lium, gallium, arsenic and various transition metals are also known to substitute into zeolite-like architectures. Some of these, such as titanium (and of course aluminium), are viewed as reactive sites that impart important properties to the zeolites. For example, titanosili­ cates are excellent oxidation catalysts73,74. The difference between a useful substituent that imparts chemical activity on the frame­ work as a whole and one that offers the chance for further synthetic modification is, of course, dependent on the conditions. For a useful catalytic material, it is preferable to retain the substituent within the framework, thus enabling full cycling of the catalyst without loss of activity, whereas for the synthetic manipulation it will often be the case that full removal of the substituent is the favoured outcome (as described above).
Some elements, such as Be and As, have obvious toxicity draw­ backs, but any time that a substituent is incorporated into the framework there is a change in chemistry, which opens up the possibility of chemical manipulations of the type that have been described for boron and germanium above. This assumes that the substituent atom introduces a weakness to the structure that can be exploited under the right conditions. To our knowledge there are no examples of such an approach using any of the elements in the previous paragraph, but there is some evidence of controlled detitanation73. Zinc, however, looks like a particularly promising example for future work. This is because it is known to promote the formation of small rings in a similar manner to germanium, for example the promotion of three-rings in the VPI-n series of materi­ als75. Given that there is therefore the potential that the zinc shows some regioselective preference in these systems, a similar synthetic strategy to those adopted for germanosilicate synthesis could be of great interest. The key to success in this area is to control both the amount and location of the substitution into the framework and then to understand the conditions needed to exploit any weakness while not disrupting the remaining framework.
An alternative approach is to alter the basic chemistry of the framework as a whole by changing the basic composition. A major class of zeotype material is the aluminophosphates. These solids have silicon replaced by equal parts aluminium and phosphorus. Many topologies are the same as the silica zeolites, except that oddnumbered rings are generally disallowed; but they seem to suffer from the same zeolite conundrum as silica zeolites. Substitution into aluminophosphates by other tetrahedral elements is possible as for silica zeolites, but again there seems to have been little or no work on manipulating structures by exploiting any weakness. This is an area ripe for further study.
Why might these synthesis methods be important?
It is clear that chemical (or other) weakness in a material can be used to prepare unique new materials; the new MOFs and zeolites prepared using these approaches have generally displayed interest­ ing properties. But the big question that remains to be answered is how fundamentally different these approaches are from tradi­ tional synthesis methods, and stemming from this, whether they can lead to materials that lie in areas of the energy–density plot that are currently not accessible (Fig.  1b). To access the as-yet unex­ plored areas of Deem’s energy–density diagram, we will need new synthetic approaches that are not limited to forming structures at the low-density edge of the distribution. The ADOR process looks to be one excellent possibility for achieving this: although the par­ ent zeolite may well be limited to the low-density edge, the subse­ quent manipulations can move the final structure off the red line (Fig. 1b). Indeed, in silico studies completed by Nachtigall and coworkers76,77 show that many potential products of the ADOR pro­ cess lie well away from the red line and also contain many more large odd-membered rings, in keeping with Deem’s prediction. The energies of these materials are marked on Fig.  4b. The key then is how to target these materials. Figure  4a shows one possibility using the ADOR method. The initial hydrolysis of a material can lead to layered intermediates. Any interactions between the layers could then be broken by swelling, and the layers then de-swelled in different orientations using organic structure-directing agents (shown in Fig. 4a as different coloured shapes). If such structuredirecting agents can be found that organize the layers into suitable orientations, final calcination of the solids will form new materi­ als. Many of these materials lie away from the energy–density cor­ relation (Fig.  4b). Attractively, there is also the potential of really controlling the way the layers are condensed by introducing larger groups between them, leading to larger pore materials than are currently available. There is also the possibility of preparing new types of hybrid materials using these approaches. There has been much interest in, for example, inorganic–organic hybrid versions of zeolites78,79. Could these new routes lead to such materials? The argument above works just as well for ZIF and MOF materials as it does for zeolites. There are potentially even more inaccessible ZIF and MOF materials, and it is very likely that many reactions are similarly limited by the solvothermal method.
The realization that regioselective weakness in 3D materials can be used as a synthetic tool therefore not only offers the pos­ sibility of preparing materials with interesting new properties, but also offers a way to target the synthesis of many porous materials that are simply not possible using current methods. Only a small number of examples have so far been demonstrated, but there are many MOFs, zeolites and aluminophosphates that have not been explored. We look forward to seeing whether the great success of the recent studies reviewed here will be extended to other classes over the next few years.
